CRPS Type I Lower Extremity
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-fracture sympathetic mediation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-011
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe lower limb allodynia, acute swelling, and profound mobility impairment following a prior orthopedic injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, unmanageable burning pain and dramatic color changes in the lower extremity, creating an inability to weight-bear or tolerate clothing. Urgent physical assessment was necessary to rule out deep vein thrombosis or acute compartment syndrome and to implement neuro-modulating analgesia before standard hours to manage severe distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My foot feels like it is on fire, and even the bedsheet touching it sends me into absolute agony.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute flare of right lower extremity pain, evolving over 24 hours. The condition initiated weeks ago following a minor tibial fracture, but tonight progressed rapidly from a dull ache to severe burning pain. Symptoms include marked edema, altered sweating unilaterally, and profound mechanical allodynia in the foot and lower leg. The patient describes a linear deterioration where dependency of the limb causes unbearable throbbing.
Functional Status: Bedbound tonight due to extreme hyperalgesia and inability to tolerate footwear or weight-bearing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high fall risk due to unilateral functional loss and severe pain interference.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Distressed, frequently altering limb position, actively guarding the right lower extremity.
Physical Exam: Right lower leg and foot exhibit mottled, erythematous skin changes with shiny, tight skin secondary to edema. Exquisite mechanical allodynia to light touch present over the dorsal foot. Peripheral pulses are palpable bilaterally. No calf tenderness on palpation; Homan's sign negative. Presentation demonstrates archetypal post-fracture sympathetic mediation.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is cluttered; trip hazards observed along the path to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen taken at maximum doses with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: CRPS Type I Lower Extremity - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral calf cord palpability, lack of deep muscular tenderness, and prominent superficial hyperalgesia rather than deep venous pain.

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal leukocyte response, and the pain being vastly out of proportion to the degree of erythema.

Acute Compartment Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft compartments on deep palpation, intact distal pulses, and chronicity stemming from a healing fracture rather than acute high-velocity trauma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe localized swelling, severe pain out of proportion to the original injury, and vasomotor changes. Immediate home evaluation was imperative to definitively exclude life- and limb-threatening vascular conditions such as deep vein thrombosis and compartment syndrome. Having clinically confirmed a complex regional pain exacerbation, prompt initiation of targeted sympathetically mediated pain relief was required, as standard OTC analgesics were ineffective, leaving the patient in a severe, functionally compromising pain crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of Celecoxib 200mg daily and commenced Amitriptyline 10mg nocte to modulate sympathetic nerve firing. Targeted intervention strategy includes COX-2 inhibitors combined with low dose Cannabinoids and Vitamin C.
Positive Behavioral Change: By engaging in 5 minutes of focused visual mirror therapy with your unaffected leg tomorrow, you will actively remap your brain's perception of the injured limb, directly decreasing the burning nerve pain and rewarding yourself with increased tolerance to light touch.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to specialized pain physiotherapy clinic.
DME/Safety Optional: Over-bed cradle to keep sheets off the affected limb tonight.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to assess medication efficacy and coordinate specialist pain clinic referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if the limb becomes completely pale and cold, loses all pulse sensation, or if you develop a sudden high fever with spreading red streaks.

